  "u5-ns12": passage("u5-ns12", "Women, work, and family",
    "Industrialization changed family life unevenly by class. In working-class families, women and children often worked for wages in mills, in other people's homes as domestic servants, or at piecework at home, and young unmarried women made up much of the workforce in textile mills in Britain, the United States, and later Japan. Middle-class families, by contrast, increasingly held up an ideal of separate spheres, in which men earned money in public life while women managed the home and raised children. This ideal kept many middle-class women out of paid work, but it also led some of them into charity, education, and reform. Women organized to gain property rights, access to universities, and the vote. New Zealand became the first self-governing country to give women the vote in national elections, in 1893."),
  "u5-ns13": passage("u5-ns13", "Revolutions compared",
    "Historians often compare the revolutions of the Atlantic world between 1775 and 1825. All drew on Enlightenment arguments about natural rights and popular sovereignty, but they changed society to very different degrees. The American Revolution created a republic but left slavery in place in the southern states. The French Revolution abolished noble privileges, executed the king, and for a time abolished slavery in the colonies, before Napoleon took power and restored it. In Saint-Domingue, enslaved people rose up in 1791, and the revolution there ended slavery and created the independent state of Haiti in 1804. In Spanish America, creole elites, people of European descent born in the Americas, led the wars of independence. They ended rule from Spain, but in most of the new states, landowning elites kept their power, and Indigenous people and people of African descent gained little."),
  "u5-ns14": passage("u5-ns14", "A divided world economy",
    "By 1900 the world economy was more connected and more unequal than it had been in 1750. Industrial states in western Europe and North America produced most of the world's manufactured goods and owned most of its railways, steamships, and telegraph cables. Many other regions specialized in exporting raw materials and food: Argentine beef and wheat, Brazilian coffee, Chilean nitrates, Egyptian and Indian cotton, Malayan tin. Foreign investors, especially British ones, paid for railways and ports in Latin America and Asia that mostly ran from the interior to the coast, carrying exports out. Some historians argue that coal near the surface and access to land and resources in the Americas gave Britain decisive advantages. Others stress that, before about 1800, the richest parts of China and India had been as productive as the richest parts of Europe, so the gap between industrial and nonindustrial regions was new."),
